From 6817ed95d9418b6a17db0d2723a3d5b8fa5870e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joeri Van Dooren <ure@on6ure.be>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 14:07:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] BUSY reads a radio's sloped passband as clear, and says why
 it came on

The tone detector learns the receiver's noise shape only from blocks it
reads as clear, and until it has 64 of them it tests the raw spectrum:
the strongest bin over 270 times the 14th weakest of the band. A
passband that slopes or rolls off inside the band (1 dB per 100 Hz is
enough) is that from the first block, so BUSY came on at once, no block
was ever clear, nothing was learned and BUSY stayed on. Behind an
X6100's filter, on a dummy load, it stayed on for 5 minutes, and Pat,
which waits for a clear channel, never called.

The learning stays as it is, with a warm-up beside it: the first 64
blocks over the VU gate without a signal of their own (a VARA signature;
a carrier, in a Hann-windowed spectrum the strongest bin 20 dB over the
third weakest of the bins 3 to 8 away from it; the station's own key or
link; a block only the VU's hang lets through) are kept whatever the raw
test says. If the clear blocks have not taught the shape by then, it is
taken from those: each bin's lower quartile of ln power, plus the
quartile's distance under the mean for noise (exponential power, 0.669),
which a signal there less than three quarters of the time does not
reach. From there the learning goes on as before, and the whitened test
reads the slope as clear, about 4 s after the start.

Busy::why() says what tripped the detector, and the HF station logs it
before each BUSY ON it did not force itself: "busy: a tone at 1406 Hz",
with "(the noise shape not learned yet)" before the shape is known,
"busy: a VARA signal (level 5)"; and once, when the warm-up gave the
shape, "busy: the receiver's noise shape taken from its first blocks,
too few were clear".

Tests: noise falling 1 and 1.5 dB per 100 Hz clear within 5 s, and a
carrier on it busy within 3 blocks; a carrier there from the start, a
signal there half the time and a VARA link there from the start not
taken for the passband; a rolled-off passband through the station, its
reports in order. Without the warm-up the passband tests and the
station's fail, BUSY on all the time.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
